abstract: >-
  Incentre lattice: a construction (incentres of the pyramids of a polytope, joined into a contact framework) with interactive
  explanations and replayable checks. The main results concern the bridged cross-polytope framework (the contact framework of
  the truncated n-cubic honeycomb): with unit springs on all bars, C11/C12 = (n-1)^2 and the Poisson ratio along a cube axis
  is nu_100 = 1/(n^2-n-1) for every n >= 3; and in three dimensions an exact continuous motion from the cab contact framework
  to face-centred cubic packing that keeps every bar length, with non-overlap and strictly increasing density (0.2233 to
  pi/sqrt18) over the whole parameter interval. Both are PROVEN (the motion by written argument and an exact finite
  certificate), with internal review only. Supporting results: sampled bar-preserving paths to hcp and fcc close packing in
  the 2x2x2 and 1x1x1 cells (VERIFIED) with exact end states (PROVEN). Secondary results: the n-dimensional zero-mode and
  self-stress counts (recorded as lemmas), a radius-ratio identity and the maximal packing fraction of the CaB6 structure
  type. Claims are labelled PROVEN (written proof), VERIFIED (finite check, not a proof), KNOWN (cited), NEW-COMPUTATION
  (own computation, not independently reproduced) or CONJECTURE (proposed, not established).
  Code is MIT licensed; text and figures are CC BY 4.0. Parts were produced with AI assistance; see CONTRIBUTORS.md.
keywords:
  - rigidity theory
  - contact frameworks
  - sphere packing
  - radius ratio
  - hexaboride
  - reproducible research
